# Line endings, pinned — and the reason is a gate, not a preference.
#
# M0's gate is "`nem web-check` passes on a clean checkout". Three of the checks
# it runs are *drift* checks: the token pipeline, the generated API client and
# the honesty table each regenerate their artefact and compare it byte for byte
# against the committed file. Each generator writes LF, because Prettier does.
#
# Without this file, a clone on a machine with `core.autocrlf=true` — the
# Windows default, and what this repository is developed on — checks those
# artefacts out with CRLF. The generator then produces LF, the comparison fails,
# and `nem web-check` reports drift on a checkout nobody has touched. The gate
# fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the thing it was written to
# protect, which is the worst kind of failing gate: it teaches people to ignore
# it.
#
# So text is normalised to LF in the index and checked out as LF everywhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated artefacts, named explicitly. They are the files whose byte-identity
# is load-bearing, and naming them means the rule survives somebody relaxing the
# wildcard above.
frontend/src/design/generated/** text eol=lf
frontend/src/generated/**        text eol=lf
frontend/src/public/generated/** text eol=lf
backend/nemesis/api/openapi.json           text eol=lf
backend/nemesis/api/api_contract_lock.json text eol=lf
backend/nemesis/events/schema_lock.json    text eol=lf

# Windows entry points. `nem.cmd` is run by cmd.exe, which is the one consumer
# in this repository that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary — never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.wasm   binary
*.zip    binary
*.onnx   binary
*.safetensors binary
